From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 11:52:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/8] Consume the handle a host function returns

handleHostCall() returned qjs_dup_value(result.ptr) and never freed the
handle the callback returned, so each call of a host function leaked
that handle: its box, and the reference it held (an object returned by
a host function stayed alive). A plugin runtime making many host calls
grew its memory by about 3 MB per 200k calls.

The returned handle is now consumed: its box goes to the trampoline as
is, which takes the value out and frees the box, and the handle reads as
disposed (disposing it again, or the end of an enclosing scope, is a
no-op). Handles the host keeps using are duplicated instead: the cached
singletons (vm.undefined, vm.null, vm.true, vm.false, vm.global), the
borrowed this/arguments of the call, and ephemeral functions, whose
disposal unregisters their callback. A callback may return nothing,
which returns undefined. Returning a disposed handle (a cached handle
returned twice), a handle of another VM or a non-handle throws into the
guest with a message saying what to do.

HostFunction documents the rule. Deferred.settled throws a clear error
if read after its promise handle was disposed or returned, instead of
using the freed box.
